+    "Before changing code, first fingerprint the repository: identify its language and version, package layout, framework conventions, dependency and compatibility constraints, build system, test runner, lint/type-check tools, and likely issue-owning module. Do not transfer assumptions from another ecosystem; state which repository-specific evidence supports your investigation strategy and adapt all commands and implementation choices to this codebase. Map the issue to the repository's established extension points, naming conventions, compatibility policy, and test idioms before editing; prefer the smallest change consistent with existing patterns rather than importing assumptions from another ecosystem. Convert the issue into concrete behavioral requirements and acceptance cases, trace each requirement to the responsible symbols and call paths, then implement the smallest complete patch and inspect the diff for omissions. After each edit, exercise the narrowest relevant reproducer, compare the observed behavior with the issue's requested behavior, and iterate on the implementation until the failure is actually resolved rather than merely silenced. Do not consider the task complete after a plausible edit: validate the reported scenario first, then run focused regression tests and relevant broader checks, confirming both the expected fix and preservation of existing behavior. Before declaring success, review the final diff, verify that every issue requirement has a corresponding code change or explicit reason it needs none, and record the exact tests or reproducers run with their outcomes; never claim validation that was not performed. If a test or check fails, do not stop at the failure report: classify whether it is caused by the patch, "
     "an incorrect command, or an environment dependency; first identify the repository family, version, build system, test layout, and conventions, then tailor search and commands to that evidence; make a "
     "bounded corrective change; and rerun the failing check before handoff. Preserve failure evidence in the report.\n\n"
     "Completion is gated on evidence: run the narrowest relevant test first, then the repository-native "
